1. Cached Firebase Instances

Before: Called FirebaseAuth.getInstance() and getCurrentUser() repeatedly in onBindViewHolder
After: Cache these in constructor and reuse them
Impact: Eliminates repeated expensive getInstance() calls

2. Cached User Data

Before: Called auth.getCurrentUser().getUid() multiple times
After: Cache user and UID once in constructor
Impact: Avoids repeated null checks and method calls

3. Extracted Methods

Before: All logic crammed in onBindViewHolder
After: Split into openBlogDetails() and setupBookmarkFeature()
Impact: Cleaner code, easier to maintain, better performance

4. Simplified Logic

Before: Complex conditional statements
After: Streamlined with ternary operators and boolean flags
Impact: Fewer CPU cycles per bind operation

5. Optimized ViewHolder

Before: Non-final fields
After: Final fields for better JVM optimization
Impact: Minor performance gain through compiler optimizations

6. Better Position Handling

Before: Used holder.getPosition() which can return invalid positions
After: Use holder.getAdapterPosition() with null check
Impact: Prevents crashes and unnecessary updates

7. Early Returns

Before: Null check in middle of method
After: Early return at method start
Impact: Avoids unnecessary processing for invalid data

Performance Benefits:

✅ Faster scrolling - Less work per item bind
✅ Reduced memory allocations - Cached references
✅ Fewer database calls - Optimized Firebase usage
✅ Better responsiveness - Simplified UI updates
✅ Cleaner code - Separated concerns

Result: Your RecyclerView will scroll smoother and consume less resources!